from <math> import exp, log, log1p, log10, pow, sqrt;

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  Photoresistor — CdS light-dependent resistor (LDR)
//
//  light_in IS IN LUX
//  ──────────────────
//  The illuminance on the cell, in lux, read as the voltage of this node
//  (V(light_in) = E, so 1 V = 1 lux). It is only read — nothing is stamped on
//  it — so it must be driven: a voltage_source used as a light level, or a
//  behavioural block. Negative light is read as darkness.
//
//  Note that this is NOT the unit of the other optoelectronic models: the
//  phototransistor (and so the optocoupler) reads light_in as OPTICAL POWER IN
//  WATTS, and an LED's light_out is scaled to suit that. LDR data sheets are
//  photometric — every number on them is given at so many lux of standard
//  illuminant A (2856 K) — so lux is the unit the cards can be checked
//  against. Wiring an LED's light_out straight into an LDR's light_in is
//  therefore a unit error, not a light path: convert first (lux = W/m^2 of
//  the source's spectrum times its luminous efficacy, ~683 lm/W at 555 nm,
//  divided over the lit area).
//
//  Bright light and `.op;`: a light node is an ordinary node at E volts, and
//  the operating-point solver moves a node at most 0.05 V per iteration
//  (500 per source-stepping rung), which reaches at most ~250 V on the
//  0.1-alpha rungs. So a deck whose light level at t = 0 is above ~250 lux
//  gets "OP FAILED — continuing from an unconverged bias", with or without
//  `.op;` (the bias-point solve runs either way). The transient itself is
//  fine at any light level: it pins the light node on its first step and the
//  cell then adapts at its own speed, as a real one would. To start a bright
//  scene adapted, begin it at <= 250 lux, or let the run settle first.
//
//  Static law
//  ──────────
//  A CdS cell is a resistor whose conductance follows illuminance as a power
//  law, R = R10 * (E / 10 lux)^(-gamma), in parallel with a dark leakage:
//
//      G_ss(E) = 1/Rdark + G1 * (E / 10)^g
//
//  G1 and g are solved from the card so that the TWO data-sheet points come
//  out exact, dark leakage included: R(10 lux) = R10 and R(100 lux) =
//  R10 / 10^gamma (gamma is defined on data sheets as lg(R10/R100)). Without
//  that correction a 1 MOhm dark leakage would pull a 14 kOhm R10 down by 1.4 %.
//  The parallel conductance saturates smoothly at Rdark in the dark — there
//  is no clamp in this file, so every derivative Newton sees is the true one
//  (see the header of <semiconductors/diode> on why that matters).
//
//  Dynamics: slow, and slower to recover
//  ─────────────────────────────────────
//  CdS cells respond in tens of milliseconds and recover in the dark more
//  slowly than they light up (trap emptying). The model carries an internal
//  state
//
//      u = ln(G * Rdark)     (u = 0 dark-adapted, ~4.3 at 10 lux for R10/Rdark
//                             = 1/71, ~7 at 1000 lux)
//
//  which relaxes toward its static value u_ss(E) = ln(G_ss(E) * Rdark) as a
//  first-order lag, with one time constant while u is rising (light came on)
//  and another while it is falling (light went off). Lagging the LOG of
//  conductance gives the familiar shapes: a sigmoidal, delayed rise from
//  darkness, and a decay that is fast in the first few percent and then
//  creeps toward the dark resistance.
//
//  The state is the voltage of an internal node s, held by a capacitor C and
//  a current C * (u - u_ss) * k, so du/dt = (u_ss - u) * k: a real state of
//  the circuit, integrated by the active solver like any capacitor, and
//  initialised by `.op;` to the operating point (the cell adapted to the
//  light at t = 0). WITHOUT `.op;` a transient starts from u = 0, i.e. from a
//  fully dark-adapted cell — which is what u = ln(G*Rdark) was chosen for.
//
//  C is 1 uF rather than 1 F, and that is not cosmetic. C cancels out of the
//  dynamics but sets the size of the row's residual, and the transient's
//  trust-region Newton accepts a step only if the residual norm falls. When
//  the light steps out of darkness, (E/10)^g has an enormous slope at E ~ 0,
//  so the first iterates put this row's residual far above the light
//  source's own (10 V for a 10-lux step); at 1 F the trust region then
//  refused to move the light node at all and the run stopped at the step
//  ("convergence blocked by row main.light"). At 1 uF the row weighs in at
//  microamps and the same step converges. Its accuracy is unaffected: the
//  step test on u is in volts, and u is held to the node tolerance.
//  (That was measured while the transient tried the trust region first. Since
//  2026-11-14 it tries plain Newton first and falls back to the trust region;
//  whether 1 F would pass today has not been re-measured. 1 uF is right
//  either way: it keeps this row's residual on the scale of the circuit's.)
//
//  The rising/falling choice is a smooth blend, not an if: with d = u_ss - u,
//      w = (1 + d / sqrt(d^2 + 0.01^2)) / 2      (1 rising, 0 falling)
//      k = w / tau_r + (1 - w) / tau_d
//  The rate (u_ss - u) * k is continuous either way (it is zero at d = 0);
//  the blend also makes its derivative continuous. Its width, 0.01 in ln G,
//  is 1 % of conductance — invisible at any timing a data sheet specifies.
//
//  t_rise, t_decay: what the numbers mean
//  ──────────────────────────────────────
//  The card takes the DATA-SHEET times, and the time constants above are
//  derived from them, under the conventional photocell definitions (the GL55
//  sheets do not spell theirs out; these follow Hamamatsu's CdS cell sheets):
//
//    t_rise    a dark-adapted cell is lit with 10 lux: the time for its
//              conductance to cover 63 % (1 - 1/e) of the change from the
//              dark to the final 10-lux value.
//    t_decay   the 10 lux is removed after the cell has settled: the time for
//              its conductance to fall by 63 %, i.e. to 37 % (1/e) of the
//              way from dark to the 10-lux value.
//
//  A log-domain lag covers those 63 % in conductance in a fraction of a time
//  constant that depends on the step's size in ln G (u_f = ln(Rdark/R10)):
//      tau_r = t_rise  / ln(u_f / (u_f - ln(1 + 0.632*(Rdark/R10 - 1))))
//      tau_d = t_decay / ln(u_f / ln(1 + 0.368*(Rdark/R10 - 1)))
//  For a GL5528 (20/30 ms, R10 14.1k, Rdark 1M) that is tau_r ~ 8.9 ms and
//  tau_d ~ 115 ms. A step to or from a different illuminance follows the
//  same two time constants; real cells are somewhat faster in bright light
//  and much slower after a long dark rest, neither of which is modelled.
//  Light flickering faster than the cell (a lamp at 100 Hz) settles nearer
//  the bright end of the swing, since the cell lights up faster than it
//  recovers — a real CdS effect, here with the model's two constants.
//
//  "Dark resistance" on a CdS data sheet is not a static value: the cell
//  keeps climbing for minutes after the light goes. Data sheets quote it at a
//  stated time (GL55: 10 s after removing 10 lux), and the model treats
//  Rdark as the value it settles to — it is reached well within those 10 s
//  (GL5528: 95 % of it 0.5 s after the light goes).
//
//  The resistor itself is non-polar: i = (V(a) - V(b)) * G flows from a to b
//  for either sign, and G >= 1/Rdark > 0 keeps the matrix regular even
//  in the dark.
//
//  Not modelled: temperature (the GL55 curve rises ~0.3 %/K below 25 C and
//  flattens above), spectral response
//  (a card is valid for illuminant A), light history beyond the single lag,
//  noise, and the voltage/power ratings, which are only documented on the
//  part cards. Parameters:
//
//    R10      Ohm  resistance at 10 lux (after light adaptation)
//    gamma         lg(R10/R100): the log-log slope between 10 and 100 lux
//    Rdark    Ohm  dark resistance; must be > R10
//    t_rise   s    rise time,  as defined above
//    t_decay  s    decay time, as defined above
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

analog module Photoresistor<double R10, double gamma, double Rdark,
                            double t_rise, double t_decay>()
    [wire a, wire b, wire light_in]
{
    // ── Static law: fitted through R(10) = R10 and R(100) = R10/10^gamma ────
    double gd  = 1.0 / Rdark;
    double g1  = 1.0 / R10 - gd;
    double gex = log10((pow(10.0, gamma) / R10 - gd) / g1);

    // Smooth positive part of the light. Its floor at E = 0 is 5e-7 lux,
    // which adds ~0.3 % of a GL5528's dark conductance; below zero it falls
    // as 1/|E|. The floor exists because (E/10)^g has an infinite slope at
    // E = 0 — the derivative must stay finite where darkness begins.
    double e_raw = V(light_in);
    double e_pos = 0.5 * (e_raw + sqrt(e_raw * e_raw + 1.0e-12));

    // u_ss = ln(G_ss * Rdark) = ln(1 + (g1/gd) * (E/10)^g)
    double u_ss = log1p(g1 * Rdark * exp(gex * log(e_pos / 10.0)));

    // ── Rise/decay time constants from the data-sheet times ────────────────
    double a1    = Rdark / R10 - 1.0;
    double u_f   = log1p(a1);
    double tau_r = t_rise  / log(u_f / (u_f - log1p(0.6321205588 * a1)));
    double tau_d = t_decay / log(u_f / log1p(0.3678794412 * a1));

    // ── The lagging state u = V(s): du/dt = (u_ss - u) * k ──────────────────
    wire s;
    C<1u>() [s, gnd];

    double u  = V(s);
    double d  = u_ss - u;
    double w  = 0.5 * (1.0 + d / sqrt(d * d + 1.0e-4));
    double k  = w / tau_r + (1.0 - w) / tau_d;
    double i_s = -1.0e-6 * d * k;       // 1e-6: the capacitor's 1 uF (header)
    current_source<>(i_s) [s, gnd];

    // ── The resistor ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // exp(u) continues as its own tangent above u = 30 (Rdark / 1e13: no
    // cell gets there; 1e5 lux is u ~ 10). Only Newton iterates do: the
    // first iterate after a step out of darkness linearises (E/10)^g at
    // E ~ 0, where its slope is enormous, and can land u far out —
    // u's own row is linear, so the next iterate is right, PROVIDED this one
    // did not overflow to inf. Value and slope are continuous at the seam.
    double g = gd * exp(u);
    if (u > 30.0) { g = gd * exp(30.0) * (u - 29.0); }
    double i_r = (V(a) - V(b)) * g;
    current_source<>(i_r) [a, b];
}
